# Linux

A practical Linux project and learning repository for exploring open-source operating systems, command-line tools, system administration, and software development.

Linux is a family of open-source operating systems used everywhere—from personal computers and servers to phones, cloud infrastructure, containers, and embedded devices.

## Overview

This repository is intended as a starting point for Linux users and developers. It can include scripts, configuration examples, exercises, documentation, and tools that demonstrate how Linux systems work.

## Features

- Explore Linux commands and shell workflows
- Experiment with Bash and shell scripting
- Learn basic system administration
- Organize configuration and automation examples
- Practice using Git and GitHub from the terminal

## Getting Started

### Requirements

- A Linux distribution such as Ubuntu, Fedora, Debian, Arch Linux, or Linux Mint
- A terminal emulator
- Bash or another supported shell
- Git

On Debian/Ubuntu-based distributions, install Git with:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install git
```

### Clone the Repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/kaktinsmartins13-wq/Linux.git
cd Linux
```

## Repository Structure

The structure may grow as the project develops. A typical layout might include:

```text
.
├── scripts/       # Shell scripts and automation examples
├── configs/       # Configuration examples
├── docs/          # Notes and tutorials
└── README.md      # Project documentation
```

## Basic Linux Commands

```bash
pwd             # Print the current directory
ls              # List files
cd /path        # Change directory
mkdir example   # Create a directory
cp file copy    # Copy a file
mv old new      # Rename or move a file
rm file         # Remove a file
cat file        # Display file contents
man command     # Read a command's manual page
```

## Contributing

Contributions are welcome.

1. Fork the repository.
2. Create a descriptive branch:

   ```bash
   git switch -c feature/my-change
   ```

3. Make your changes and test any scripts.
4. Commit and push the branch.
5. Open a pull request describing the change.

Please use clear commit messages and keep documentation accurate. Include comments in scripts where the purpose or behavior may not be obvious.

## Helpful Resources

- [Linux.org](https://linux.org/) — Linux learning resources
- [Kernel.org](https://www.kernel.org/) — Official Linux kernel source and releases
- [Linux manual pages](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/) — Command and system interface documentation
- [The Linux Command Line](https://linuxcommand.org/) — Free command-line guide
- [GNU Bash Manual](https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/) — Bash documentation

